    text: `The bus was crowded when I left work on Tuesday. I was tired, and I was happy to find the last empty seat near the door.|At the next stop, an older man entered with two heavy bags. He held the rail, but the bus moved suddenly and he almost fell.|I saw him, yet I waited for someone else to stand. For a few seconds, I told myself that my day had also been difficult.|Then a schoolboy across from me started to rise. He was carrying a large backpack and looked more tired than I felt.|I became ashamed. I stood first and offered my seat to the older man. He thanked me and placed his bags beside his feet.|The boy smiled, and I smiled back. Nothing dramatic happened, but my mood changed at once.|I understood that good manners often begin before anyone asks us for help. We have to notice people and act without waiting for praise.|The man later asked where I worked. We spoke quietly about the busy road and the rain that was coming.|Before his stop, he thanked me again. I told him it was a small thing, and he said small things can make a hard journey easier.|His words stayed with me. I had nearly protected my comfort while another person struggled in front of me.|Now, when I enter a bus, I look around before I sit. If someone needs the seat more, the choice becomes simple.|Respect is not only polite language. Sometimes it is a seat, a door held open, or a moment of attention.|That short ride taught me to notice first, decide quickly, and help with a sincere heart.`,
