    text: `Last month, my cousin Hana visited our home after a long journey. I wanted to hear every detail about her new job and apartment.|As soon as she entered, I started asking questions. Was the train late? Did she like her manager? Was her neighborhood safe?|Hana answered politely, but her voice was quiet. My mother noticed her tired face and gently touched my arm.|She welcomed Hana, carried one of her bags, and showed her where to wash. Then she brought water, warm tea, and a simple meal.|No one asked Hana to tell a story while she ate. We gave her time to breathe and become comfortable.|After resting, Hana joined us in the living room. This time, she spoke with energy and shared funny moments from the journey.|Later, my mother explained something important. A guest is not a performer who must entertain us as soon as they arrive.|Welcoming a person means thinking about what they need. A traveler may need water, food, privacy, prayer, or quiet before conversation.|I realized that my questions were friendly, but my timing was not considerate. Good intentions still need good manners.|The next weekend, another relative came to our house. I greeted him warmly, took his coat, and showed him the bathroom first.|I offered a drink and said he could rest before joining us. His shoulders relaxed, and he thanked me.|That small change made our home feel calmer. I also enjoyed the conversation more because our guest was ready for it.|Now I remember my mother's simple rule: welcome the person first, and ask the questions later.`,
