    text: `Rafi, can I ask you about something from this morning's meeting?|Of course. Did I forget to send you one of the notes?|No, the notes were clear. It is about our new colleague, Naila. You called her Nadia three times.|Oh, I did not realize that. The names sounded similar in my head, and nobody corrected me.|She looked uncomfortable, but she did not want to interrupt you in front of the whole team.|I feel terrible. I should have checked her name before I introduced her.|Mistakes happen. I wanted to tell you privately so you can correct it without feeling embarrassed.|Thank you. That was kinder than announcing it across the office.|What will you do now?|I will speak to Naila before lunch. I will say her name correctly, apologize briefly, and not make excuses.|That sounds good. You could also correct the meeting notes because her name appears there twice.|You're right. I will update the file and send the corrected version to everyone.|Do you think I should make a long apology in the group chat?|Probably not. A simple correction protects her from more mistakes without making her the center of attention.|I understand. I want her to know that remembering her name matters.|It does. A person's name is a basic part of showing respect.|Thank you for telling me quietly, Lina. You helped both of us today.|And thank you for listening calmly. Some people become defensive when they are corrected.|I was embarrassed for a moment, but the important thing is fixing the mistake.|Exactly. Kind correction and a sincere response can keep a small error from becoming a bigger problem.`,
