    id: 'a1-akhlak-09', title: 'Owning the Broken Cup', level: 'A1', themeId: 'akhlak',
    text: `At my first office job, I often used a blue cup from the shared kitchen. It belonged to my coworker Sinta.|One afternoon, the cup slipped from my wet hand and broke on the floor. Nobody else was in the room.|For a moment, I wanted to clean the pieces and say nothing. There were many cups, and perhaps Sinta would not notice.|But her name was written underneath it. I remembered that she used the cup every morning and had brought it from home.|I swept the floor carefully, placed the broken handle in a paper bag, and went to her desk.|I told her exactly what happened. I did not blame the wet sink or say the cup was already weak.|Sinta looked surprised, then thanked me for being honest. The cup was a gift from her sister, so she felt sad about it.|I apologized and offered to replace it. She said a new cup would not be the same, but she accepted my apology.|That answer taught me something. Taking responsibility does not remove every result of our mistake.|Sometimes we cannot return what was lost. We can only tell the truth, say sorry, and try to repair what we can.|The next day, I brought a simple new cup and a small note. Sinta now keeps it beside her computer.|We later laughed about how carefully I hold every cup in the kitchen. Still, I am glad I spoke immediately.|Hiding the accident might have protected me for one day, but it would have weakened trust between us.|Honesty was uncomfortable for one minute. A lie or silence could have remained much longer.`,
