    text: `Last Friday, I waited forty minutes for a repair worker who had promised to come at nine. I had changed my work schedule for him.|When he finally arrived, he did not apologize. He only asked where the broken washing machine was.|Anger rose quickly inside me. I wanted to speak sharply so he would understand that my time mattered.|Then I noticed his hands were shaking slightly. His shirt was wet from the rain, and he looked worried.|I took one slow breath and asked whether everything was all right. His face changed immediately.|He explained that his motorcycle had stopped on the road. His phone battery was dead, and he had pushed the motorcycle to a small workshop.|He knew he should have contacted me, but he had no way to do it. He apologized and offered to return another day if I was busy.|I was still unhappy about the delay, yet the full story made a difference. I told him we could continue.|He repaired the machine carefully and showed me how to prevent the same problem. Before leaving, he apologized again.|Controlling anger did not mean pretending the delay was acceptable. I still told him that customers need information when plans change.|The difference was my manner. I spoke clearly without trying to hurt or humiliate him.|Afterward, I felt grateful that I had paused. A harsh first reaction would not have repaired my machine or his motorcycle.|Anger often gives us only part of a story. One calm question can open the rest.|That morning was a quiet win. Nobody praised me, but I left the conversation with fairness and self-respect.`,
