    text: `My apartment used to become messy very quickly during the working week. Shoes stayed near the door, cups filled my desk, and papers covered the table.|I usually ignored everything until Saturday. Then I spent half the day cleaning and felt angry about losing my weekend.|A friend suggested a fifteen-minute evening reset. The idea sounded too small, but I decided to try it.|Every night after dinner, I set a timer. First, I return objects to their places and put dirty clothes in the basket.|Next, I wash the cups and plates I used that day. I do not clean every shelf or start a large job.|During the last five minutes, I prepare for tomorrow. I fill my water bottle, check my bag, and choose simple clothes.|When the timer rings, I stop. This is important because the routine should feel light enough to repeat.|On the first night, the apartment was not perfect. After four evenings, however, the difference was easy to see.|My mornings became calmer because my keys and bag were ready. Saturday also became a real day off instead of a cleaning day.|Sometimes I miss the reset because I come home late. I do not punish myself or try to do thirty minutes the next night.|I simply begin again the following evening. A useful routine should support life, not become another reason for guilt.|The best part is the feeling when I wake up. The room is quiet, the table is clear, and I can start without searching for things.|Fifteen minutes cannot solve every household problem, but it can stop small messes from growing.|For me, a short daily habit works better than one exhausting cleaning session each week.`,
