    text: `At the beginning of May, I checked my bank account and wondered where my money had gone. The answer was sitting in my office bin.|Every workday, I bought lunch, a drink, and sometimes a snack. Each purchase seemed small, but together they cost a lot.|I decided to bring lunch from home for one month. I did not plan beautiful meals or buy special boxes.|On Sunday, I cooked rice, chicken, and vegetables for two days. On other evenings, I packed part of our family dinner.|The first week was difficult because I often forgot the box in the refrigerator. I placed a note beside my front door.|The note said, “Lunch, keys, phone.” It looked silly, but it worked. I also kept a spoon at my desk.|By the second week, packing lunch felt normal. I tried simple changes, such as adding fruit, soup, or a boiled egg.|I still ate with my coworkers. Bringing food did not mean sitting alone or refusing every invitation.|On one Friday, we visited a small restaurant together. I enjoyed it because it was a planned choice, not a daily habit.|At the end of the month, I compared my spending. I had saved enough to pay an important bill without worry.|There was another benefit. My afternoon energy was better because I chose lighter food and knew what was inside it.|I learned that saving money did not require a perfect budget. One clear daily change made a visible difference.|I now buy lunch once a week and bring it on the other days. This balance is realistic for me.|The small box in my bag is now part of my morning routine, and my bank account is calmer too.`,
