    text: `My alarm used to ring at six, but I rarely stood up before half past six. I pressed the snooze button again and again.|Those extra minutes did not make me feel rested. They made me rushed, and I often left home without breakfast.|Last week, I tested a simple change. I put my phone on a shelf across the room before sleeping.|When the alarm rang, I had to leave the bed to stop it. On the first morning, I nearly climbed back under the blanket.|Instead, I opened the curtains and drank the glass of water beside my desk. The light helped me wake up.|I had prepared my clothes and bag the night before. Because of that, I did not need to make many decisions.|The second morning was still hard, but I had time to eat eggs and toast. I arrived at work ten minutes early.|By Thursday, I noticed that my evenings also needed to change. Waking early is difficult when I watch videos until midnight.|I began charging my phone outside the bedroom at ten thirty. Then I read a few pages of a book and slept.|On Friday morning, I woke a minute before the alarm. That felt surprising after years of fighting the snooze button.|One week does not mean the habit is permanent. I know there will be cold mornings when the bed feels especially comfortable.|Still, I now understand that the solution is not stronger motivation at six o'clock. It is better preparation the night before.|A distant phone, ready clothes, water, and an earlier bedtime make the morning choice easier.|My goal is not to become a perfect morning person. I simply want to begin the day without panic.`,
