    text: `In my evening English class, I understood most lessons, but one grammar point confused me for three weeks.|Each time the teacher asked whether we had questions, I looked down. Everyone else seemed ready to continue.|I was afraid my question was too simple. I copied examples from the board and promised to study them alone.|At home, the examples still made no sense. I completed exercises by guessing and felt more worried each week.|One Tuesday, the teacher gave us a short task. I made the same mistake in every sentence.|She did not criticize me. She placed a small mark beside the first answer and asked me to visit her desk after class.|I expected a long explanation, but she used two colored pens and one clear example. Suddenly, I saw the difference.|Then she said that several students had made the same mistake. They had also been quiet when she invited questions.|The next lesson, I raised my hand. My voice felt weak, yet I asked her to explain one instruction again.|Two classmates nodded with relief. One of them thanked me during the break because he had been confused too.|That moment changed how I see questions. A question is not proof that a student is slow or unprepared.|It is information for the teacher. It shows where the lesson needs another example, a slower step, or different words.|I still try to solve problems independently first. I read my notes and attempt the exercise.|But if I remain confused, I now ask. Ten honest seconds in class can save an hour of guessing at home.|Learning became easier when I stopped trying to look perfect and started trying to understand.`,
