    text: `Mariam, would you like to study together for the language test next month?|Maybe. I enjoy studying with friends, but our last group talked more than it studied.|I remember. Six people were too many, and nobody had a clear plan.|What kind of study session are you suggesting this time?|Only the two of us, twice a week, for forty-five minutes after work.|That sounds realistic. What would we do during those forty-five minutes?|For the first ten minutes, we can review vocabulary and test each other without looking at notes.|Then perhaps we could practice one listening exercise and compare our answers.|Good idea. We should explain why we chose each answer, not only check the answer key.|I also need speaking practice. Could we finish with a short role-play each time?|Yes. We can use situations from the course, such as visiting a clinic or asking for directions.|Where should we meet? The café near work is convenient, but the music is sometimes loud.|The public library has a small discussion room. We can reserve it online for free.|Perfect. I want us to agree on one more thing: if either person is tired, we still begin on time.|Agreed, but we can choose a lighter activity instead of canceling the whole session.|I like that. A short useful meeting is better than waiting for a perfect evening.|Shall we try Tuesday and Thursday for two weeks, then review whether it helps?|Yes. I will reserve Tuesday's room, and you can choose our first listening exercise.|Deal. I feel calmer already because the plan is specific.|Me too. A good study partner is helpful, but a clear routine makes the partnership work.`,
